1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,460
Every year, thousands of Americans die due to pharmaceutical drug mishandling.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:09,340
Dr. Marv Shepard, the former chairman of the pharmacy administration at the University

3
00:00:09,340 --> 00:00:14,640
of Texas, claims that the typical pharmacy makes two to four mistakes a day, which is

4
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:17,060
an alarming rate for such a high-stakes action.

5
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:22,340
By developing and training our own edge-compute model and integrating other machine learning

6
00:00:22,340 --> 00:00:27,240
features, we were confident we could create an automated system to reduce error and increase

7
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:29,300
public confidence in pharmacists.

8
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:34,280
With tools like PharmaSuite, we hope to build a new tomorrow where everyone can trust health

9
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:35,060
care professionals.

10
00:00:35,755 --> 00:00:42,555
PharmaSuite minimizes drug handling errors by monitoring all activities relating to prescription drug handling within a pharmacy.

11
00:00:43,655 --> 00:00:52,275
From the moment an item is shipped to the pharmacy, PharmaSuite keeps track of the quantities, expiration dates, and locations of every drug and pill bottle.

12
00:00:53,095 --> 00:01:02,895
When a pharmacy technician makes a transaction, throws away expired pills, or picks up a new product, PharmaSuite's MongoDB database is automatically updated.

13
00:01:03,935 --> 00:01:12,755
If a pharmacist attempts to place a pill bottle on the wrong shelf, or sell an expired product, they are alerted and the transaction is blocked.

14
00:01:13,835 --> 00:01:18,095
Replay data is collected for each transaction to maximize transparency.

15
00:01:19,255 --> 00:01:24,495
Whenever a pharmacy is low on stock, PharmaSuite will automatically ask providers for new shipment.

16
00:01:25,535 --> 00:01:30,755
Through the full stack, almost all possible sources of error in a pharmacy would be automated.

17
00:01:31,733 --> 00:01:34,553
AI was integrated into our project on every level.

18
00:01:35,173 --> 00:01:39,793
The shipment data was converted into stock information by the meta API ecosystem, which

19
00:01:39,793 --> 00:01:43,553
then used the information to create bounding boxes for where the drugs should be placed

20
00:01:43,553 --> 00:01:46,133
in a way that minimizes the chance of mix-ups.

21
00:01:48,575 --> 00:01:51,455
To prevent the model from invading the privacy of pharmacy technicians,

22
00:01:52,035 --> 00:01:55,415
PharmaSuite only begins tracking the pharmacists when they're making a transaction.

23
00:01:56,235 --> 00:02:00,895
For the next part of our project, we train a custom edge compute model using data from a wearable

24
00:02:00,895 --> 00:02:05,855
inertial measurement unit on the pharmacist's wrist to identify when the pill bottles are picked up or placed.

25
00:02:06,655 --> 00:02:11,255
By collecting 5,000 data points manually, we ensured the neural network could distinguish between idle,

26
00:02:11,955 --> 00:02:15,915
random, putting down, and picking up actions with 98.21% accuracy.

27
00:02:16,635 --> 00:02:21,635
Using YOLO, a computer vision model, we correlate the position of the pharmacist's arm to bounding

28
00:02:21,635 --> 00:02:26,735
boxes to track the movements of pills. After the transaction, it automatically registers changes

29
00:02:26,735 --> 00:02:30,695
in the database, ensuring that the pharmacist returns the pills into the right location.

30
00:02:31,875 --> 00:02:36,755
Through this process, PharmaSuite can make pharmaceutical handling safer and more

31
00:02:36,755 --> 00:02:41,855
trustworthy than ever before for patients while also preserving the privacy of workers.
